WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.099
Good morning John.

00:00:01.099 --> 00:00:04.892
In 2007, a group of people got together and
we hacked YouTube. There's a lot of ways to

00:00:04.892 --> 00:00:08.210
put it but I think that's the simplest. We
used all the tricks that we'd learned as YouTubers

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:11.820
to help our videos get more views, and we
used those tricks more than we would ever

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.860
have used those tricks normally. And while
this would have felt underhanded and maybe

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:19.560
even a little bit dirty if we were just doing
it to grow our video views, instead, if felt

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.170
awesome because we were doing it for charity.
Unfortunately, Mike Huckabee's attempts to

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:27.400
hack the republican nomination for president
interfered with our taking over the entire

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.610
first page of the most discussed page, but
in the end, our hack was more successful than

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:32.610
his.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:35.020
The project continued annually, although every
year seeming a little bit less subversive,

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:38.380
a little bit less underground and a little
bit more legitimate, and more and more YouTube

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:41.960
actually wanted to work together with us to
promote it. I'm like "YouTube! What's up?

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:45.249
That's not what it's about. You're supposed
to be some faceless monolith that we have

00:00:45.249 --> 00:00:48.769
to work through the back doors to get to the
top. You're not supposed to be like 'Hi my

00:00:48.769 --> 00:00:52.000
name is Ramia, and this is Margaret and Ben
and Chris and we're all gonna help you get

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.840
to the front page of YouTube and you don't
have to subvert us, we want to help'". What

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.299
I just realized John, is often the most elegant
hacks have an element of social engineering.

00:01:00.299 --> 00:01:05.140
The real con relies on somebody weaseling
their way in the organization so that they

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:10.369
can do stuff from the inside. And let me tell
you, we weaseled our way like no other. We

00:01:10.369 --> 00:01:14.090
went from having to use every trick in the
book to get a few more thousand views on YouTube,

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:18.170
to YouTube literally banging down our door
to get involved.

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:23.939
Why is that? Well, I think it's two reasons.
One, because I think everyone wants to help

00:01:23.939 --> 00:01:27.440
and they're just looking for a way. Like we
all know that the world is broken but it's

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:32.280
just so hard to figure out how to get involved
without feeling really, just depressed about

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.929
the whole thing. People want it to be meaningful
but they don't want to feel bad about it.

00:01:34.929 --> 00:01:38.479
They don't want to hate their entire lives
just because they're trying to do something

00:01:38.479 --> 00:01:41.359
good and I think that everybody knows that
that's what the Project For Awesome's for.

00:01:41.359 --> 00:01:45.689
Really the obvious and real reason why YouTube
wants to be involved in this is because they

00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:49.570
see how powerful this community is and how
much more powerful it could be if we all shared

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:53.689
these resources and used the power of our
community and of their platform and mixed

00:01:53.689 --> 00:01:56.219
it together for true epicness.

00:01:56.219 --> 00:02:00.229
So honestly, what we're doing here, is hacking
for charity harder than ever before. We hacked

00:02:00.229 --> 00:02:04.409
WheezyWaiter's chest hair, we hacked John's
fear of heights, and we hacked YouTube from

00:02:04.409 --> 00:02:10.000
the inside out. I mean how else could we change
the logo, get 40 front page video features

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.440
and have access to a team of YouTube employees
to plan a ridiculously fancy live show.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:17.970
And yeah, I think next year, we're gonna do
it a little bit differently, but there is

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:23.800
no denying that this has been the most successful
Project For Awesome ever. 600.,000 comments,

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:29.490
on over 3000 Project For Awesome videos with
a total running time of over 40 hours and

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:34.230
total video views of over 10 million. And
whereas previous Project For Awesomes had

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:38.270
been all about raising awareness through creation
of videos, this year, we also raised money.

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:45.590
Over $100,000 in small donations and a total
of over $130,000. That of course meaning that

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:51.300
the vast majority of that money was raised
by small donations from individuals like you.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:57.330
Thousands of individuals giving what they
can, adding up to a huge, huge number. I think

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:01.020
I'm proud of the Project For Awesome more
than I'm proud of anything else that I've

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.020
ever done.

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:05.250
The quality of the videos produced for the
project keeps going up, the total number of

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:08.520
people working on it keeps going up, and I'm
so impressed by the way people work together

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:12.510
cause they want to work together and they,
everyone feels they're sort of equal part

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:17.870
in this thing. And more than anything, I feel
like we are all people being people no matter

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:21.820
where we are or what our circumstances are
which I think has implications way beyond

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:22.820
the Project For Awesome.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:27.690
Thank you to all of you. Thank you John, and
John, I will see you on Wednesday.

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:30.950
End screen time! There was one video that
didn't get a lot of promotion during the Project

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:35.540
For Awesome because it was uploaded a little
late, but man are you going to weep. Shawn's

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:40.370
Uncultured Project video this year--you should
click and check it out right now.

